<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beaufort Castle, Lebanon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qal'at al-Shaqif</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beaufort Castle is a crusader fortress in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, standing on a cliff high above the Litani river. Held by the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> from 1139, it was surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1190 after a long siege, returned to the crusaders in 1240 and sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> in 1260, and was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> in 1268. In modern times it was a strongpoint in the Lebanese conflicts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beaufort-castle-lebanon.jpg" alt="Beaufort Castle, Lebanon" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ElysianEzryn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_ruins_of_Beaufort_Castle_in_2022..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Beaufort, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> for beautiful fortress, was the name the crusaders gave the castle; its Arabic name, Qalʿat al-Shaqīf, means Castle of the Steep Cliff. Little is known of the site before 1139, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-king-of-jerusalem">Fulk, King of Jerusalem</a>, captured the fortification there and gave it to the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>. The medieval historian Hugh Kennedy suggests that the crusader castle was begun soon after.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187 Beaufort was one of the last castles to resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. Arab sources tell how its lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reynald-of-sidon">Reynald of Sidon</a>, asked for three months to bring his family out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and used them to repair and stock the castle. The siege that followed ended in August 1189, when Saladin lifted it to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>. In April 1190 the garrison handed the castle over in return for Reynald's release. A treaty negotiated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-i-of-navarre">Theobald I of Navarre</a> returned it to the crusaders in 1240, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-grenier">Julian of Sidon</a> sold it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> in 1260, and in 1268 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan Baibars captured it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The castle stands on a cliff 300 metres high that falls steeply eastwards to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litani-river">Litani River</a>, about one kilometre south-south-east of the village of Arnoun in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. It is roughly triangular, about 150 metres by 100, and divided into two wards, one on the lower ground to the east. A square keep of about 12 metres a side stands against the west wall of the upper ward and, as is usual in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, is entered from the ground floor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the Ottoman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1516 the castle was held for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> by the Shiite Sa'b family. Fakhr-al-Din II took it in the early 17th century, and the Ottomans, after defeating him, destroyed its upper parts. Jazzar Pasha, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, besieged it in 1782 and destroyed many of its remaining fortifications, and the earthquake of 1837 added to the damage. Surveys began in 1880 and 1881, and Paul Deschamps studied it from 1927.</p><p>Its view over southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and northern Israel made it a strongpoint in later conflicts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">Palestine Liberation Organization</a> held it from 1976, and Israeli forces took it on 8 June 1982; the Israeli army used it as a base until 2000. In 2026 Israeli forces struck the remains during the war in Lebanon and captured the castle on 31 May; the Israeli military said it wanted to control the area around a tunnel it said <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a> used. In July 2026 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated the Mount Amel Castles, Beaufort among them, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> and classified them as endangered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beaufort Castle, Lebanon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Geoffrey’s younger son William II of Villehardouin completed the conquest by taking Monemvasia in 1248 after a long siege, and built the fortresses of Mistras, Grand Magne and <b>Beaufort</b> to control the Mani and the Slavic Melingoi of the Taygetus range.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The lordship included the coast between Beirut and Tyre and inland castles such as <b>Beaufort</b> above the Litani.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-sidon">Lordship of Sidon</a></li></ul></section>
